  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 133 PM MDT Thu Aug 20 2026\n\nA warmer and drier airmass is taking hold today as the ridge\nstarts to amplify over the Four Corners into the Great Basin. We\nstill have enough moisture per current satellite imagery for a few\nshowers and weaker storms in the mountains, mainly south of I-70.\nThose will end early this evening as the airmass stabilizes, \nwhile the plains will remain too dry and stable for any convective\nactivity. Overnight lows will be several degrees above normal. \n\nOn Friday, we'll be back to the frying pan as the thermal ridge\nshifts eastward and centers itself across Colorado. 700 mb\ntemperatures warm a couple more degrees C, which supports highs in\nthe mid to upper 90s across the plains and I-25 Corridor, with a\nfew spots nearing 100F from downtown Denver to Greeley and Fort \nCollins. The record for tomorrow in Denver is 99F set in 2023, \nand we should be very close to that at the official reporting \nstation. Those readings also bring us very close to Heat Advisory\ncriteria, but most locales should stay just below 100F. There will \nbe some heat relief in the form of high based convective clouds \nlate in the day as mid/upper level moisture starts to spread into \nthe region. Given large DCAPE of 1500-1700 J/kg and deep \ninverted-v profiles, strong microburst winds to 50+ mph will be\npossible from any high based showers. \n\nFor the weekend, ensembles are still showing the upper level ridge\naxis to shift slowly eastward or flatten, with the flow aloft\ntrending more westerly. This will allow for an uptick in monsoonal\nmoisture across the forecast area, leading to scattered thunderstorms\neach afternoon. In these moisture and flow patterns, the most \nnumerous storms are expected over the mountains and eastern \nplains. Meanwhile, the majority of the storms along the I-25 \nCorridor will be higher based as the airmass is still quite warm \nand low level moisture is expected to be lacking. But given the\nuptick in precipitable water to near 150% of normal, that still \ndoesn't mean we can't see locally heavy rainfall. \n\nThere may be some change in this pattern as flow turns slightly \nmore west-northwesterly toward Tuesday or Wednesday. That could \nallow for a weak backdoor front or two with increasing low level \nmoisture, but plenty of uncertainty exists here with respect to \ntiming and intensity of any surge(s). In any case, it appears \ntemperatures will settle a little closer to (still above) normal \nfor this weekend through much of next week. And enough moisture \nwill be around to keep a chance of storms in the forecast most \ndays.",
